  "response": "'스트레스'라는 한 단어로 일 년 넘게 이어진 잠 못 자는 밤과 두통을 설명해버리는 게 답답하셨을 것 같습니다. 특히 원인을 정확히 짚고 넘어가야 마음이 놓이는 성격이신데, 그걸 더 구체적으로 말해달라고 해도 돌아오는 답이 같았다면 더 막막하셨을 것 같아요.\n\n그리고 '여기 와야 할 사람인지 모르겠다'고 하셨는데, 꼭 진단이 나오거나 극심한 상태가 아니어도 올 수 있는 곳입니다. 다만 그건 제 말이니, 직접 느껴보시면서 판단하시면 됩니다.\n\n혹시 잠이 안 올 때나 두통이 올 때, 그 순간에 떠오르는 생각이나 상황 같은 게 기억나는 게 있으세요? '스트레스'라는 큰 틀이 아니라, 구체적으로 어떤 순간에 그런 느낌이 오는지부터 함께 짚어볼 수 있을 것 같습니다.",
  "reasoning": "The user is a counselor responding to a client who has come to therapy. Let me analyze the client's message:\n\n1. The client is uncertain whether they belong here - they're not sure if they're \"the kind of person who should come to counseling\"\n2. They've had insomnia for over a year and frequent headaches\n3. They've been to internal medicine multiple times, tests showed nothing, doctors said it's stress\n4. They came here because of that\n5. They have a personality that needs to pinpoint exact causes - so the vague \"stress\" explanation feels frustrating and dismissive\n\nKey emotions: frustration, feeling dismissed by the \"stress\" label, uncertainty about whether they belong here, a need for precision and clarity.\n\nWhat would be helpful here:\n- Acknowledge their frustration with the vague \"stress\" explanation\n- Validate that their need to understand causes is legitimate, not something to pathologize\n- Gently address their uncertainty about whether they belong here without being dismissive\n- Not jump to solutions or reframe too quickly\n- Show I'm listening to what they actually said\n\nI should avoid:\n- \"I understand how you feel\" type generic empathy\n- Jumping to \"let's explore your stress\" which would repeat the very thing that frustrated them\n- Being too cheerful or reassuring\n- Asking multiple questions\n\nLet me craft a response that:\n1. Acknowledges the frustration of having a complex experience reduced to one word\n2. Validates their need for precision without treating it as a problem\n3. Addresses the \"do I belong here\" uncertainty gently\n4. Perhaps offers a way forward that respects their need for specificity\n\nI'll write in Korean, natural counselor voice, concise.\n",
